From e036ff2378ea9bc2753b1fbb786d24081b6a3b2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Sat, 19 Sep 2026 17:13:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] fix: make the real(16) runtime byte layout endian-neutral

lf_float128 was documented and implemented as a little-endian byte array:
f128_unpack, f128_pack_parts, lf_float128_to_str and the sign flips read
and wrote bytes[0..7] as the low half and bytes[15] as the sign byte. But
lfortran_intrinsics.c fills the struct with memcpy from the program's
fp128 values and the compiler-rt wrappers convert with memcpy too, so on
a big-endian target such as PowerPC every value was read with its halves
swapped: PRINT of 1.0_16 produced 0.000000000000029859651965415290707E-4933.

Define lf_float128 as the value's native memory image instead, and route
every access to its halves through lf_f128_to_words()/lf_f128_from_words()
in the shared header, which pick the byte order from __BYTE_ORDER__. The
codegen side, which turns a kind=16 RealConstant into an APInt, uses the
same helper so compiler and runtime agree on any host. Little-endian
targets are unchanged: the helpers reduce to the previous memcpy pairs.

Verified with a struct-API harness that feeds native binary128 images
through to_str/from_str, arithmetic, sign and comparison helpers: built
with powerpc-linux-gnu-gcc 14 and run under qemu-ppc it fails 20 of 20
checks before this change and passes after; on x86-64 it passes before and
after, as does a __float128 harness exercising the public entry points.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
